2026-09-03 11:06:24 [info     ] model response: # Expectations
The criterion requires that the submission includes a correct implementation of the Sparse Hopper environment (a variant of the MuJoCo Hopper environment with sparse rewards, as described in the paper, specifically based on Mazoure et al. (2019)). The paper states: "Under the sparse reward setting, the reward informs the x position of the hopper only if x>0.6 in our experiments." The environment should be a proper Gym environment that can be initialized and used to run the experiments. I would expect to see code that either defines a custom Gym environment or modifies the standard Hopper-v3 environment to implement this sparse reward structure. This could be a wrapper or a new environment class. The submission must contain actual executable code (not just instructions) that creates such an environment.

# Reality
I examined all the provided files:

- `Refine_mujoco/environment.py`: Defines `RetrainEnv`, a wrapper for resetting to critical states, but does not modify the reward to be sparse. It uses `gym.make(env_name)` internally, so it simply wraps the standard dense environment.
- `Refine_mujoco/retrain.py`: Uses `make_retrain_env` with `--env "Hopper-v3"`, which calls `gym.make("Hopper-v3")`. No sparse reward logic is present.
- `Refine_mujoco/retrain.sh`: Passes `--env "Hopper-v3"`, again the dense version.
- `Refine_mujoco/baseline/train.py`: Uses `gym.make("Hopper-v3")` for training a baseline agent, no sparse variant.
- `Refine_mujoco/masknet/environment.py`: Also uses `gym.make(env_name)` without any sparse reward modification.
- `Refine_mujoco/utils.py`: Contains helper functions but no environment definition.
- `README.md`: Mentions implementation in "four dense/sparse mujoco games", but the code provided contains no sparse environment implementation.

There is no file that defines a sparse Hopper environment (e.g., a wrapper that gives reward only when x > 0.6). The submission only uses the standard dense Hopper-v3 environment. Therefore, the criterion is not met.

